body:
## Goal

Wire the `Document` parser wrapper into the browser window, so that fetching a real page shows its **title** (in the window title bar) and its **text content** (in the label) — instead of the first 500 characters of raw HTML.

This is the first end-to-end user-visible milestone: network → parse → display. It's Phase 2 of the return-to-ADR-001 plan.

Builds on:
- Phase 0 (#91) — lexbor vendored and building
- Phase 1 (#93) — `Document` wrapper with `parse()`, `title()`, `text_content()`

## Current State

In `src/browser/window.cpp`, `on_url_activated()` ends with:

```cpp
constexpr std::size_t kMaxPreview = 500;
std::string preview = r.body.substr(0, kMaxPreview);
if (r.body.size() > kMaxPreview) {
    preview += "\n\n... اقتُطع المحتوى";
}
m_label.set_text(preview);
m_label.set_justify(Gtk::Justification::LEFT);
m_status_label.set_text(
    "تم الجلب، الحجم: " +
    std::to_string(r.body.size()) + " بايت");
```

This is the placeholder ADR-006 explicitly says to replace.

## Design

Do **not** call `Document::parse()` directly from `window.cpp`. That would put a `renderer/html` include into the GTK layer, and any parsing bug would only be reachable by launching the browser and pointing it at a URL — untestable in CI.

Instead, extract a small, testable helper into `honeybee_lib`:

### New file: `src/renderer/html/preview.hpp`

```cpp
#pragma once

#include <cstddef>
#include <string>
#include <string_view>

namespace honeybee::renderer::html {

// A short, presentation-ready view of an HTML document.
struct Preview {
    std::string title;  // <title> content, empty if none
    std::string text;   // visible text, truncated to max_text bytes
};

// Parse `html` and produce a Preview.
//
// - If parsing fails (only happens on allocation failure), returns an
//   empty Preview with the title "Parse error" and no text.
// - `max_text` truncates the extracted text. Truncation is byte-based
//   for now (no UTF-8 awareness) — a later phase can improve this.
Preview make_preview(std::string_view html,
                     std::size_t max_text = 1000);

} // namespace honeybee::renderer::html
```

### New file: `src/renderer/html/preview.cpp`

Implementation:

1. Call `Document::parse(html)`.
2. If `nullptr`: return `{"Parse error", ""}`.
3. Otherwise: `{doc->title(), truncate(doc->text_content(), max_text)}`.
4. Truncation: if `text.size() > max_text`, cut at `max_text` and append `"..."`.

That's it — ~40 lines including comments.

### Where it goes

Add `renderer/html/preview.cpp` to `honeybee_lib` in `src/CMakeLists.txt`, alongside `document.cpp`.

## Changes to `src/browser/window.cpp`

Replace the `substr` block with:

```cpp
#include "renderer/html/preview.hpp"

// ...inside the fetch callback, on success...

const auto preview =
    honeybee::renderer::html::make_preview(r.body);

// Window title: "<page title> — HoneyBee" if we have a title,
// otherwise leave the default title alone.
if (!preview.title.empty()) {
    set_title(preview.title + " — HoneyBee");
}

m_label.set_text(preview.text.empty()
                     ? "(empty page)"
                     : preview.text);
m_label.set_justify(Gtk::Justification::LEFT);

m_status_label.set_text(
    "تم الجلب، الحجم: " +
    std::to_string(r.body.size()) + " بايت");
```

Notes:
- If the page has no `<title>`, we don't touch the window title (leave the default set in the constructor).
- If the extracted text is empty (e.g. a page that's all `<script>`), show a placeholder rather than a blank label.
- The byte-size status message stays — it's useful.

## Tests

Add `tests/test_preview.cpp` and register it in `tests/CMakeLists.txt`. Cover:

1. **Simple page** — `<html><head><title>Hi</title></head><body><p>Hello</p></body></html>` → `title == "Hi"`, `text` contains `"Hello"`.
2. **No title** — `<html><body><p>Hi</p></body></html>` → `title` empty, `text` contains `"Hi"`.
3. **Truncation** — text longer than `max_text` is cut and ends with `"..."`.
4. **Custom max_text** — pass `max_text = 10`, verify truncation at 10 bytes.
5. **Malformed HTML** — no crash, returns something sane.
6. **Empty input** — returns an empty-ish preview (title empty, text empty).
7. **Parse failure fallback** — hard to trigger directly (lexbor rarely fails); document that the fallback exists. Optionally test that `make_preview` never throws.

## Acceptance Criteria

- `src/renderer/html/preview.{hpp,cpp}` exists.
- `window.cpp` no longer uses `r.body.substr(0, 500)`.
- Window title bar updates to show the page's `<title>` after a successful fetch.
- Label shows extracted text (not raw HTML).
- All existing tests still pass.
- New preview tests pass.
- `honeybee_lib` still has no GTK dependency.
- CI passes.

## Manual Verification

After merging, run:

```
./build/bin/honeybee
```

Fetch `https://example.com` (or type a public URL). Expected:
- Window title becomes **"Example Domain — HoneyBee"**
- Label shows the text "Example Domain ... This domain is for use ..." etc.
- Status bar still shows the byte size

## Out of Scope

- DOM node classes (Phase 3).
- Layout, CSS, painting (Phase 4–5).
- Replacing the `Gtk::Label` with a Cairo drawing area (Phase 5).
- Smart truncation (word-aware, UTF-8-aware) — the current byte-based version is fine for now; revisit in a later phase.

## Learning Outcomes

- Extracting testable logic out of a GTK callback.
- Designing a small value-type return (`Preview`) instead of out-parameters.
- Using the parser wrapper end-to-end for the first time.
- Handling null/failure without exceptions.

## Dependencies

- Blocked by: nothing (Phase 1 merged in #93).
- Blocks: nothing directly, but this is a prerequisite for meaningful Phase 3+ work (there's no point building a DOM layer if the window can't display its output).
